our converge toward 1.6-2.0% real over a decade of 100%+ debt/GDP, that is +0.45 to +0.55 pp on the weighted average with the US unchanged at 2.60%. Add panelist 2's +0.40 real term premium (price-sensitive levered basis-trade accounts as the marginal duration holder, eroding convenience yields, 3-yr P95 Debt-at-Risk 117% of GDP +2.4 pp under a 20% AI equity shock, equity-bond correlation flipping positive so duration must pay for itself) and the locked (r-g) worsening of +1.0 pp.\n\nRepression wedge: I cut it to -0.10 (0.25 probability x -0.40 pp). Reinhart-Sbrancia repression was executed on CAPTIVE domestic banking systems with closed capital accounts and a central-bank bid. The 2030s G7 has open capital accounts, a leveraged hedge-fund marginal buyer, and index-constrained foreign ownership — the regulatory-captive channel (LCR, Solvency II, pension matching) is real but an order of magnitude smaller than the 1945-1980 machinery. All three seats imported the base rate without checking whether its mechanism is installed.\n\nP50 = 1.83 - 0.05 (net r*, adopted) + 0.40 (term premium) + 0.20 (non-US convergence, net of the part already in the premium) - 0.10 (repression) = 2.28 -> 2.3%.\n\nBAND, deliberately WIDER than all three. P90 = 3.6 (vs 3.1 / 3.3 / 3.1): a fiscal-dominance term-premium repricing with Japan normalized and no repression; the 1970s->1980s precedent is a 500 bp decade shift and the three seats all scaled their right tail down on the argument that the 1980s started from a higher inflation base — but the 1980s also started from LOWER debt/GDP and a price-INSENSITIVE holder base, which cuts the other way and they did not net it. P10 = 1.1: regime D plus repression, AI-capex-bust deflation, Japan and euro reals pinned.\n\nSIMULATION: not identified. capital.interestRate is an economy-wide MPK (5.99-6.96% across scenarios), there is no 10y sovereign benchmark, no termPremium in src/, no breakeven. Zero point estimate taken; I accept panelist 2's directional use of the cross-scenario debtRiskPremium ordering (tech-breakthrough lowest at 1.20 pp, debt-populism highest at 2.50 pp) as weak support for an upper-half answer, and nothing more. Low-fertility-conditional, and on this window that conditioning is growth-flattering and therefore yield-flattering UP.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.649Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"by_when\":\"IMF WEO October 2029 edition\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ3 P50 falls from 2.1% to ~1.8% and P90 to ~2.9%; CQ6-A drops from 25 to ~14 with the mass going to B (+8) and C (+3). This kills my measured-vs-true-China argument, which carries ~0.3 pp of my CQ3 and the bulk of my A.\",\"signpost\":\"IMF WEO prints combined 2030 and 2031 China real GDP growth below 3.5%/yr (not a forecast - the realized/estimate line for those years), i.e. the official series stops printing near 4.5%.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Q4 2029 OECD CPI release\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ2 falls from 48 to ~32, CQ6-C from 27 to ~18, CQ6-D rises from 6 to ~13. This falsifies the PPP-composition argument that is the single load-bearing plank of my CQ2 and of my unusually low D.\",\"signpost\":\"OECD-wide GDP-weighted core inflation (OECD's own published aggregate) averages below 2.30%/yr for four consecutive quarters, with Turkiye's core in single digits and Mexico's below 3.5%.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ5 falls from 30 to ~15, i.e. back through the Wave-1 centre. The MOVE level-scaling argument is my whole edge on CQ5; a sub-90 three-year average at 4%+ nominal yields refutes it empirically rather than theoretically.\",\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE three-year average over 2027-2029 prints below 90 while the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal yield averages above 3.75%.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 c